   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_ZJ82Gv6yR2XWKRT4",
   "name": "Volumes of Revolution About the x-axis",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Volumes of Revolution About the x-axis"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a volume of revolution about the x-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **solid** **of** **revolution** is formed\n\n  - when an **area** bounded by a function $y=f(x)$ and the lines $x=a$ and $x=b$\n  - is **rotated** $2π$ radians $(360^{\\circ})$ about the $x$-axis\n- The **volume** **of** **revolution** is the volume of this solid"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/06/2dk4S6Oy_6-2-4-cie-fig1-vol-of-rev.png",
   "file": "assets/d2404d7ea20a-2dk4s6oy-6-2-4-cie-fig1-vol-of-rev.png",
   "alt": "Graph rotating around x-axis to form a volume of revolution",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Be careful – the ’front’ and ‘back’ of this solid are **flat**\n\n  - they were created from **straight (vertical) lines**\n  - 3D sketches can be misleading!"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is the formula for a volume of revolution about the x-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "The **volume of revolution** of a solid rotated $2π$ radians ($360^{\\circ}$) about the $x$-axis between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by:"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- `V equals pi integral subscript a superscript b y squared space d x`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This is **not** given on the exam formula sheet, so you need to remember it\n\n  - Note that $πy^{2}$ is the **area** of the **circular cross-section** of the solid at any value of $x$\n  - That might help you remember the form of the volume integral\n- $y$ is a **function of **$x$\n\n  - i.e.  `y equals straight f open parentheses x close parentheses`\n- $x=a$ and $x=b$ are the equations of the (vertical) lines **bounding** the area\n\n  - $a<b$  ($a$ is the 'left boundary' and $b$ is the 'right boundary')\n  - $x=a$ and $x=b$ may be **given** in the question\n  - one boundary may be the $y$**-axis** ($x=0$)\n  - the $x$**-axis intercepts** of $y=f(x)$ may also be boundaries"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I calculate the volume of revolution about the x-axis?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
